  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978465/assets/210d2120771bef60b0db703a04a06df395225a0c9a884faf012d55f0ed1f60d6.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of the cubic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $5.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-4}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2,3}=3\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=5.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =5(x-1)(x-(3+2\\text{i}))(x-(3-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x-1)(x-(3+2\\text{i}))(x-(3-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 5(x-1)({x}^{2}-x(3-2\\text{i})-x(3+2\\text{i})+(3+2\\text{i})(3-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 5(x-1)({x}^{2}-6x+13) & = \\\\ 5({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+13x-{x}^{2}+6x-13) & = \\\\ 5{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}+95x-65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=5{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}+95x-65.$",
